.TH "setup-ltsp-app" "8" "2026-09-11" "ltsp6"
.SH "NAME"
setup\-ltsp\-app \(em Configure an LTSP application server and join it to the domain
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.PP
\fBsetup\-ltsp\-app\fR \fIip/cidr\fR \fIiface\fR \fIgateway\fR \fIdc\-ip\fR \fIrealm\fR \fIadminuser\fR \fIadminpass\fR [\fIhome\-server\-ip\fR]
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
Run \fIon\fR the application server, as root. The host accepts the RDP sessions
that HAProxy distributes to it, runs the desktop inside them, and
authenticates its users against the Samba AD domain. It builds and serves no
images \(em that is setup\-ltsp\-root(8)'s job. One of these runs per hypervisor
node so the load balancers have genuinely independent backends.
.PP
Run it detached. From the point where it switches to the static address, a
session over the old one is cut:
.PP
.nf
setsid nohup setup\-ltsp\-app 172.17.250.31/20 ens18 172.17.240.1 \
    172.17.250.10 LTSPLAB.LOCAL administrator '<admin\-password>' \
    172.17.250.40 > /root/setup.log 2>&1 &
.fi
.PP
Put the password in a small script file rather than on the command line if
process listings and shell history matter.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
.IP "\fIip/cidr\fP" 4
Static address. HAProxy's backend list names it, and the machine account in AD
is tied to this host's name and address.
.IP "\fIiface\fP \fIgateway\fP" 4
Interface and default gateway.
.IP "\fIdc\-ip\fP" 4
Domain controller, written first into /etc/resolv.conf.
.IP "\fIrealm\fP" 4
Upper\-case Kerberos realm, for example LTSPLAB.LOCAL.
.IP "\fIadminuser\fP \fIadminpass\fP" 4
Credentials used once, for the domain join.
.IP "\fIhome\-server\-ip\fP" 4
Optional. When given, /home is mounted from that host over NFS, so every
application server shows the same home directories.
.SH "WHAT IT CONFIGURES, AND WHY"
.PP
Each item exists because its absence failed in a way that pointed somewhere
else.
.IP "\fBStatic address, immutable resolv.conf\fP" 4
Debian 13 ships dhcpcd started by ifupdown rather than as a systemd unit, so
\fBsystemctl is\-enabled dhcpcd\fR says \fInot\-found\fR while several dhcpcd
processes run. It rebinds a lease alongside the static address and overwrites
/etc/resolv.conf with an empty stub. The symptom is not a DNS error: sssd
quietly drops offline, after which new domain accounts report \(lqno such
user\(rq while already cached ones keep working.
.IP "\fBA [realms] block in krb5.conf\fP" 4
\fBdefault_realm\fR alone leaves kinit(1) reporting \(lqCannot find KDC for
realm\(rq, because the shipped krb5.conf knows only the MIT example realms.
.IP "\fBchrony against the DC\fP" 4
Kerberos rejects tickets more than five minutes out. A member that drifts
simply stops being able to log anyone in.
.IP "\fBethtool, then the offload unit\fP" 4
Under nested virtualisation checksum offload corrupts UDP and breaks DHCP. The
unit that turns it off was present on two servers for days while ethtool was
not installed \(em enabled, visible and permanently \fIfailed\fR. An enabled
unit is not evidence; check \fBsystemctl is\-active ltsp\-nic\-offload\fR.
.IP "\fBxe on the glamor allow list\fP" 4
xorgxrdp gates glamor behind \fBOption "DRMAllowList"\fR in
/etc/X11/xrdp/xorg.conf, which Debian ships as \fB"i915 radeon"\fR. Intel's Arc
B\-series is driven only by \fBxe\fR, never i915. Inert until xorgxrdp is built
with \fB\-\-enable\-glamor\fR, which Debian does not do \(em see NOTES.
.IP "\fBxscreensaver instead of light\-locker\fP" 4
light\-locker locks by way of the logind seat, and an xrdp session has no seat
of its own. The Xfce power manager goes too: nothing to manage on a session
server, and one polling copy per session adds up.
.IP "\fBThe orphaned\-session reaper\fP" 4
See NOTES. This one made every login fail invisibly.
.IP "\fBadcli join, not realmd\fP" 4
realmd verifies its dependencies through PackageKit, which a minimal Debian
host does not have, so it refuses the join with \(lqNecessary packages are not
installed\(rq while listing packages that are all installed.
.IP "\fBsssd with the GPO settings\fP" 4
Without \fBad_gpo_map_remote_interactive = +xrdp\-sesman\fR a correct password
still fails with \fBpam_acct_mgmt failed: Permission denied\fR: sssd's GPO
access control runs enforcing and maps every PAM service it does not recognise
to Denied, whatever AD actually says.
.SH "NOTES"
.PP
.B A successful login that ends after two seconds.
The client is told \fBERRINFO_LOGOFF_BY_USER\fR \(em \(lqthe user logged
off\(rq \(em and nothing anywhere logs an error. What happened is that an
xfce4\-session from an earlier login still holds the D\-Bus name
\fBorg.xfce.SessionManager\fR on the user bus, which lives in
/run/user/<uid> and outlives the single session. The new xfce4\-session finds
the name taken and exits 0; xrdp sees its window manager finish cleanly and
ends the session. Every link behaves correctly, which is why it is silent.
Look for \(lqexited quickly\(rq in /var/log/xrdp\-sesman.log and for a stray
process:
.PP
.nf
pgrep \-a \-u "$(id \-u \fIuser\fR)" xfce4\-session
.fi
.PP
Orphans are made by restarting xrdp\-sesman while sessions run, which is easy
to do while setting a server up. This script installs
/usr/local/sbin/xrdp\-reap\-orphan\-sessions as an \fBExecStartPre\fR of
xrdp\-sesman, where the rule is sound: before sesman starts it tracks nothing,
so any process still carrying \fBXRDP_SESSION\fR belongs to a lost session. The
same rule on a running server would be wrong.
.PP
.B The address switch must not strand the host.
An earlier version flushed the interface and left ifupdown to restore it. When
that did not happen the machine was unreachable with no way in, because /home
is a hard NFS mount and every login then blocks in the kernel waiting for a
file server it can no longer route to. It took a power cycle. The interface is
now brought down and up by name, and the static address is set directly if
ifupdown did not apply it.
.PP
.B GPU acceleration is not reachable with Debian's packages.
libxorgxrdp.so contains no glamor, EGL, GBM or DRI3 symbols, so glamor needs a
self\-built xorgxrdp with \fB\-\-enable\-glamor\fR, which upstream still calls
experimental. Keep apart what is being accelerated: glamor accelerates
rendering \fIinside\fR the session, while the RDP stream's encoding stays in
software.
.SH "VERIFICATION"
.PP
.nf
klist \-k /etc/krb5.keytab | grep \-i "host/$(hostname \-s)@"
id administrator          # same uid on every host, or /home breaks
findmnt \-no SOURCE,FSTYPE /home
systemctl is\-active xrdp xrdp\-sesman sssd
systemctl is\-active ltsp\-nic\-offload   # must be active, not failed
.fi
.PP
Do not grep the keytab for the hostname alone: adcli also writes
\fBRestrictedKrbHost\fR entries, so a plain hostname match reports \(lqalready
joined\(rq for a host that never joined.
